The front face is a mana engine; the back face is the deck. The Prismatic Bridge puts a creature or planeswalker from your library onto the battlefield at the beginning of every upkeep — free, uncastable, uncounterable — and this deck is built out of six- and seven-mana planeswalkers precisely because it never intends to pay for them. Everything else either protects the Bridge, doubles the loyalty, or untaps the walkers.

# The commander
Esika, God of the Tree :: cast the front face for {G}{G} when you need mana and fixing: she taps for any colour and gives your other legendary creatures the same ability.
Cast The Prismatic Bridge when you need the engine. A free planeswalker every upkeep wins the game on its own if it survives two turns, and nothing in a normal Commander game answers an enchantment quickly.
Oath of Nissa :: digs three for a creature, land or planeswalker, and it lets you spend any colour on planeswalker spells — which in a five-colour deck matters more than it looks.

# Doubling the loyalty
Doubling Season :: planeswalkers enter with twice the loyalty, which means most of them can ULTIMATE the turn they arrive. Elspeth, Garruk and Chandra all end games immediately.
Oath of Teferi :: blink a permanent on arrival, and then activate a loyalty ability twice each turn on every walker you control.
Ichormoon Gauntlet :: every planeswalker gains "[0]: proliferate" and "[−12]: take an extra turn", and every noncreature spell adds counters. It turns a stalled board into a chain of extra turns.
Atraxa, Praetors' Voice :: proliferate at every end step, so every walker ticks up an extra time for free.
Commodore Guff :: a loyalty counter on another walker each end step, plus bodies and card advantage.
Rings of Brighthearth :: copies any loyalty ability for {2}. Two activations, one counter spent.
Carth the Lion :: digs seven for a planeswalker whenever he enters or a walker dies, and your walkers' abilities cost one less loyalty to activate up and one more to tick down.

# The planeswalkers
Elspeth, Sun's Champion :: three bodies a turn, a one-sided wrath against big creatures, and an emblem that ends the game.
Garruk, Apex Predator :: kills a walker, kills a creature, makes a 3/3, and the ultimate is lethal.
Liliana, Dreadhorde General :: a Zombie a turn, a symmetric edict, and a card for every creature you lose.
Chandra, Awakened Inferno :: uncounterable, and the emblems accumulate so the table simply burns down.
Jace, the Mind Sculptor :: card selection, a Brainstorm every turn, and a bounce for the creature attacking your walkers.
Narset, Parter of Veils :: opponents draw one card a turn. Against a blue table that is close to a lock.
Oko, Thief of Crowns :: turns their best permanent into a 3/3 Elk, or makes Food while ticking up.
Nicol Bolas, Dragon-God :: has every loyalty ability of every other walker on the battlefield, which in this deck is an enormous menu.
Tamiyo, Field Researcher :: draws off combat, taps blockers, and the ultimate casts spells free.
Lord Windgrace :: card advantage and land recursion on a walker that protects itself.

# Finding the pieces
Demonic Tutor :: any card.
Enlightened Tutor :: any artifact or enchantment — The Prismatic Bridge is not tutorable this way, but Doubling Season and Ichormoon Gauntlet are.
Arena Rector :: dies into ANY planeswalker onto the battlefield, for free.
Carth the Lion :: a planeswalker off the top seven, repeatedly.
Sensei's Divining Top :: sets your draws and the Bridge's hit.
Sylvan Library :: with this much card selection, the four-life payments are usually right.
Rhystic Study :: their turns pay for yours.
Mystic Remora :: the strongest card in the deck on turns one through four.

# Protecting the board
Planeswalkers lose to attacks, so half the deck exists to make attacking impossible.
Norn's Annex :: creatures cannot attack you OR your planeswalkers unless their controller pays two life per attacker. This is the single most important defensive card in the deck.
Teferi's Protection :: phases everything out through a wrath, a combo kill or a lethal attack.
Dawn's Truce :: hexproof for you and your permanents, and gifted it is even cheaper.
Final Showdown :: spree, so you can strip abilities and wrath in one cast while keeping one creature.
Farewell :: pick the modes that miss your own board. Against an artifact or graveyard table it is one-sided.
Elspeth, Sun's Champion :: the minus is a wrath that leaves your Soldiers alive.
Fierce Guardianship :: free with your commander out.
Force of Will :: free, for the spell you genuinely cannot let resolve.
Mana Drain :: counter, and the mana casts a seven-drop walker.
An Offer You Can't Refuse :: one mana for anything noncreature.
Cyclonic Rift :: overloaded, it clears every attacker and every answer at once.
Swords to Plowshares :: the cleanest removal in the format.
Path to Exile :: the second copy.
Anguished Unmaking :: instant-speed exile for any nonland permanent.

# Mana in five colours
Sol Ring :: turn one.
Mana Vault :: three on turn one.
Mox Amber :: live the moment any legend or walker resolves, which in this deck is constantly.
Mox Diamond :: a land for a Mox.
Chrome Mox :: fast, at the cost of a card.
Arcane Signet :: fixing.
Fellwar Stone :: usually taps for what you are missing.
Nature's Lore :: an untapped dual.
Farseek :: a shockland.
Esika, God of the Tree :: the front face turns every legend you control into a mana source of any colour.

# What to keep in an opening hand
Keep fixing and acceleration. Five colours and a six-mana commander is the deck's real cost — Sol Ring, the Moxen and the land-fetch spells are what make turn four or five possible.
Choose the face deliberately. Esika for two mana is a ramp piece when you are behind on mana; The Prismatic Bridge for six is the engine when you are not. Casting the wrong one is the most common mistake.
Doubling Season before a planeswalker, always. The same Elspeth is a value engine without it and an immediate emblem with it.
Norn's Annex :: prioritise it once you have two walkers out. Your walkers are the win condition and combat is the only thing that reliably kills them.
